# The Nobel Prize and the World's Big Awards

> Alfred Nobel's will created the most famous prize on earth, and
> general-knowledge papers never tire of its details: the six
> categories, the first laureates, the Indian names on the roll. Then
> they widen the net to the Oscars, the Booker, the Pulitzer, and the
> Magsaysay — each with its own trap. One lesson, and the awards
> section of any paper is yours.

## 1. The Nobel Machinery

[definition]
The **Nobel Prizes** were established by the will of **Alfred
Nobel** (the Swedish inventor of dynamite, d. 1896) and first awarded
in **1901** in five fields — Physics, Chemistry, Physiology or
Medicine, Literature, and Peace. The **Prize in Economic Sciences**
was added in 1968 by Sveriges Riksbank (Sweden's central bank) in
memory of Nobel, and is awarded with the others but is not one of
Nobel's original five.
[/definition]

| Category | First laureate (1901) | Indian-connected winners |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Physics | Wilhelm Röntgen (X-rays) | C.V. Raman (1930), S. Chandrasekhar (1983) |
| Chemistry | Jacobus van 't Hoff | H. Gobind Khorana (1968), Venkatraman Ramakrishnan (2009) |
| Medicine | Emil von Behring | H. Gobind Khorana (1968, shared) |
| Literature | Sully Prudhomme | Rabindranath Tagore (1913) |
| Peace | Henry Dunant and Frédéric Passy | Mother Teresa (1979), Kailash Satyarthi (2014) |
| Economics (from 1969) | Ragnar Frisch and Jan Tinbergen | Amartya Sen (1998), Abhijit Banerjee (2019) |

Every category's "first Indian" is exam currency: **Tagore (1913,
Literature — the first non-European laureate)**, **C.V. Raman (1930,
Physics — the first Asian science Nobel)**, **Mother Teresa (1979,
Peace)**, **Amartya Sen (1998, Economics)**. The diaspora names —
Khorana, Chandrasekhar, Ramakrishnan, Banerjee — were US/UK citizens
at the time of award, and papers use the phrasing "of Indian origin"
for exactly this reason.

Among the world records: **Marie Curie** was the first woman
laureate (Physics 1903) and remains the only person to win Nobels in
two *different sciences* (Physics and Chemistry). The Peace Prize can
go to organizations (the Red Cross has won it three times), while
the others go only to individuals (up to three, shared). No posthumous
award is allowed unless the laureate dies between announcement and
ceremony — a rule triggered when it emerged that Ralph Steinman had
died days before his 2011 Medicine announcement.

## 2. The Oscars and the Screen

The **Academy Awards (Oscars)** began in **1929**; the statuette's
formal name is the Academy Award of Merit. For Indian candidates the
key rows are: **Bhanu Athaiya** — first India-born winner, costume
design for *Gandhi* (1983); **Satyajit Ray** — Honorary Oscar (1992);
**A.R. Rahman and Gulzar** — Best Original Song, "Jai Ho" from
*Slumdog Millionaire* (2009); **Resul Pookutty** — Best Sound Mixing
(2009); and **"Naatu Naatu"** from *RRR* (2023), Best Original Song,
composed by **M.M. Keeravani**.

The great Oscar trap: *Slumdog Millionaire* (8 Oscars, 2009) was a
**British production** directed by Danny Boyle — "first Indian film
to win Best Picture" is false on both counts. *Parasite* (2020,
Bong Joon-ho) was the first non-English-language Best Picture, a fact
papers now use as the decoy's decoy.

## 3. The Letters — Booker, Pulitzer, and the Rest

| Prize | Since | For | Indian milestones |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Booker Prize | 1969 | Best novel in English (Commonwealth/Ireland, now open) | Arundhati Roy (1997), Kiran Desai (2006), Aravind Adiga (2008), Geetanjali Shree (2022, *Tomb of Sand* — first Hindi novel) |
| Pulitzer Prize | 1917 | American journalism and letters (Columbia University) | Jhumpa Lahiri (2000, Fiction) as an Indian-American winner |
| Ramon Magsaysay Award | 1958 | Asia's "Nobel for public service" (Manila) | Vinoba Bhave (1958, first), later Kiran Bedi, Arvind Kejriwal, etc. |
| International Booker | 2005 (novel 2016) | Translated fiction | Geetanjali Shree/Daisy Rockwell (2022) |
| Jnanpith (India) | 1965 | Indian-language literature | Covered in the national awards lesson |

The Booker trick is temporal: the prize is for a novel *published*
that year by a citizen of the Commonwealth or Ireland (the
eligibility was widened in 2014 to any English novel published in the
UK), which is why American-Indian writers appear on some lists and
not others. **Geetanjali Shree's 2022 win** is the single most
quoted recent fact — the first translation from Hindi, and with
Daisy Rockwell as the translator it took the International Booker,
not the main prize.

## 4. The Grand Slam of Other Fields

The **Fields Medal** — mathematics' highest honour, awarded every
four years at the International Congress of Mathematicians to
mathematicians **under 40** (at most four) — has never gone to an
Indian citizen resident in India, though **Akshay Venkatesh** (2018)
is of Indian origin. The **Abel Prize** (2003, Norway) is
mathematics' answer to a lifetime-achievement Nobel, with no age
limit. **Kyoto Prizes** (Japan) and the **Turing Award** (computing,
"the Nobel of computer science") complete the technical set.

In music, the **Grammy** (Recording Academy, since 1959) counts
Indian winners **Ravi Shankar** (five, including a lifetime
achievement), **Zakir Hussain**, and **A.R. Rahman** (two, for
*Slumdog*). In television, the **Emmy** has gone to **Vir Das**
(2023, for *Landing*) — one of the newest additions to the GK canon
— while the EGOT grand slam (Emmy–Grammy–Oscar–Tony) remains a
purely international trivia fact with no Indian names in the club.

[example]
A standard multi-trap: "Who was the first Indian Nobel laureate?" —
options: C.V. Raman, Mother Teresa, Rabindranath Tagore, Amartya Sen.
The answer is Tagore, 1913. Follow-up: "first Indian to win a Nobel
in the sciences?" — now the answer is C.V. Raman (1930), with
Tagore as the trap, since Literature is not a science. A third
rotation: "first Indian woman laureate?" — Mother Teresa (1979,
Peace), still the only Indian woman on the list. One roll, three
questions, each answered by reading the qualifier.
[/example]

[summary]
The Nobel: six categories (Economics added 1968/69), first awarded
1901, first laureates Röntgen (Physics) and Dunant (Peace). Indian
firsts to memorise: Tagore 1913 (first Indian and first
non-European), Raman 1930 (first in the sciences), Mother Teresa
1979 (first Indian woman), Amartya Sen 1998 (first in Economics),
Satyarthi 2014 (Peace). Beyond the Nobel: Oscars began 1929 with
Bhanu Athaiya (1983) the first India-born winner and "Naatu Naatu"
(2023) the newest headline; the Booker (1969) with Arundhati Roy
(1997) and Geetanjali Shree's Hindi *Tomb of Sand* (2022); the
Pulitzer (1917); the Magsaysay (1958, Manila, first winner Vinoba
Bhave); the Fields Medal (under-40 rule) versus the Abel Prize
(lifetime); the Grammy with Ravi Shankar and A.R. Rahman. Read the
qualifier — "first," "Indian," "sciences," "woman" — before
marking anything.
[/summary]
